Chapter: EffectLaunderLazy
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils

 Adversarial probe (effect rows stage 0). Deferral laundering: a
 def declared pure wraps an effectful call in lazy, producing a
 thunk arrow whose result type carries the effect. Forcing it via a
 let is already rejected (CDX2033), but handing the thunk to a
 generic HOF launders it: the HOF's generic result variable absorbs
 the effectful type before the argument check compares, and the
 thunk runs Console I/O inside declared-pure code. Effect rows
 stage 3b rejects: the thunk arrow carries the deferred body's
 Console row, forcing it surfaces the effect in the ambient, and
 the def boundary rejects (CDX2031). This test is the enforcement's
 regression guard.

Section: Definitions

  boom : Integer -> [Console] Integer
  boom (n) = act
    print-line-uni "lazy boom"
    n
  end

  quiet : Integer -> Integer
  quiet (n) =
   let t = lazy (boom n)
   in list-length (list-map t [1, 2])

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("result " & show (quiet 41))
  end
